### image-server

When idle it uses about 100 MB. The first request loads the model, which takes
a minute or more. It unloads after 10 idle minutes (`IMAGE_IDLE_UNLOAD`). Only
one variant is loaded at a time: `edit` serves generations, transparent output
and edits; `img2img` loads only for `image` + `strength` variations and swaps
`edit` out. Requests run one at a time, and after 4 are waiting, new ones get
`429`. mflux is pinned to commit acdfc98 (reference editing isn't in a release
yet).

**Two models, chosen per request (since 2026-10-13):** `"model": "turbo"` (Qwen-Image-2.1-Turbo,
distilled to a fixed 8-step schedule, ignores `steps`) or `"model": "base"` (Qwen-Image 2.1, `steps`
default 40). Omitted, `IMAGE_MODEL` (default `base` since 2026-10-13, at the user's request) decides. One model is resident at a time; asking
for the other reloads it (~7–9 s). `server.py` swaps mflux's linear scheduler for Turbo's sigmas only
while Turbo runs. Both snapshots (~31–32 GB each, Turbo's preview `assets/` skipped) are in the
Studio's HF cache. Measured 2026-10-13, 1024² 8-bit, same seed: Turbo 15–17 s, base 62 s, both
peak 29.5 GB. Base gives visibly better detail (background, skin, lighting); the user found Turbo's
quality not good enough for finals. The dsh tools take `model` too. Same architecture, so the
peaks below apply to both. The other timings below are the base model's at 512².

| Task | Endpoint | Body |
|---|---|---|
| Generate | `/v1/images/generations` | `prompt`, `size` (multiples of 32), `steps`, `seed`, `transparent` |
| Variation | `/v1/images/generations` | plus `image` (base64/data URL), `strength` 0.05–0.95 (higher = more change) |
| Edit / merge / restyle | `/v1/images/edits` | `images` (1–10, base64/data URL), `prompt` naming "image 1", "image 2"…, `output_resolution` (512 is fast), optional `size` |

At 512×512 in 8-bit: generate ~20 s, 2-image merge ~47 s, restyle ~12 s,
transparent ~37 s. For a style change, `edit` with "repaint image 1 as …" works
far better than `strength`, which barely restyles.

**Before `restart`, `stop` or `install`**, check `/health`. The script refuses
while `busy` or `queued` is set, because a restart cuts off whoever is
generating. Use `FORCE=1` only if the user agrees.

**Memory guard: off** (`IMAGE_MEMORY_GUARD=0`, at the user's request; it
refused too many jobs). Requests load the quantization they ask for (default
8-bit) and run; macOS swaps if memory is tight. Measured 8-bit peaks: ~21 GB
at 512², ~25 GB at 768², ~29.5 GB at 1024², ~33 GB for img2img. Avoid bf16
(~46 GB) while Splash is running. `/health` → `memory` shows the breakdown
and swap pressure. To re-enable the guard: `IMAGE_MEMORY_GUARD=1
./image-server.sh install`. Free the image model's memory immediately with
`image-server.sh unload`. The service
writes PNGs to `~/image-server-outputs` and deletes them after 7 days.

**LoRAs (since 2026-10-16):** `.safetensors` files in `~/image-server-loras/` (`IMAGE_LORA_DIR`), named by
stem, with an optional `<name>.json` (`trigger`, `description`, `scale`). `GET /v1/loras` lists them; requests take
`"lora": "<name>"` and `lora_scale` (0–2) on generations, variations and edits. Plain names only (never paths);
an unknown one gets 422 with the list. `server.py` bakes it into the loaded weights with mflux's `LoRALoader` and
`Qwen21LoRAMapping` (the edit variant has no LoRA argument of its own, but its transformer uses the same layer
names: 256/256 keys matched), so a different LoRA/scale/none reloads (~3–6 s) and the same one is reused. Memory
and speed unchanged (26.3 GB peak at 768x1024). The dsh tools pass `lora` and `lora_scale` too. Installed:
`kawya` (trigger "a photo of kawya woman").

**Training LoRAs:** `~/lora-train` holds a uv venv (torch 2.14, diffusers 0.41.0) and diffusers'
`train_dreambooth_lora_qwenimage21.py` with its "no bf16 on MPS" checks disabled (bf16 works on MPS with
torch 2.14; fp16 risks NaNs on Qwen-Image; `.orig` is the original). mflux's own `mflux-train` can't train
Qwen-Image (only Z-Image, FLUX.2, Krea 2 and ERNIE-Image). Driven from the MacBook by
`~/repos/virtual-influencer/assets/kawya-lora/train-kawya-lora.sh`: it copies the images, stops Ornith, LFM and
the image-server (recorded in `~/lora-train/<name>/stopped-services`), trains, fetches the LoRA and starts them
again. Measured 2026-10-16 (13 images, 672x512 bucket, rank 32, 1500 steps): 1.96 s/step, 49 min, ~30 GB peak,
64 MB LoRA; checkpoints every 250 steps in `~/lora-train/kawya/output/checkpoint-*/`. The tokenizer load needs
the network (a config file the HF cache lacks), so the run script sets `HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET=1`, not
`HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`. With one caption for every image, the training outfits leak into prompts that don't
name clothing.

**From dsh:** the plugin `~/.dsh/plugins/image-generate.mjs` adds two tools:
- `generate_image`: `prompt`, `size`, `steps`, `transparent`, and
  `init_image` + `strength` for variations.
- `edit_image`: `images` (1–10 workspace paths), `prompt`, `detail`
  (low/medium/high), and optional `size`.

Both save PNGs into the session's workspace (default
`generated-images/<YYYY-MM-DD>/<theme>/<title>.png` from their `theme`/`title` params, since
2026-10-16; or a `filename` relative to the workspace), and refuse any input or output path
outside it. They find the server through `config.url` on their entry in
`~/.dsh/splash.patch.yml`, which `$IMAGE_SERVER_URL` overrides; `searxng-search`
works the same way. If a tool reports `unreachable at http://127.0.0.1:…`,
neither value reached it: re-run `./install.sh` and restart dsh.

**From Pi on the Studio** (since 2026-11-09): `~/.pi/agent/extensions/image-generate.ts` (source
`clients/pi/image-generate.ts`, copied by hand, not by `install.sh`) ports both tools with the same
parameters and save layout under Pi's cwd. It calls `http://127.0.0.1:8890` (`$IMAGE_SERVER_URL` overrides);
on the Studio the loopback port answers, the Tailscale IP gives 404. Results tell the model to view the
file with Pi's `read` tool. Tested with Ornith: generate and edit both work.

The chat UI only draws images on `read_image`'s own card, so when the current
model accepts images, the result tells the agent to call `read_image` on the
saved file. The Sushi and 35B-A3B entries declare image input (`input: [text, image]`
in `splash.settings.yaml`); on the 27B and Mellum the image is saved but not shown.

