### classify-server

GLiNER2.5-Decide (fastino) is a 340M-parameter DeBERTa-v3 classifier for
operational decisions: intent routing, urgency, sentiment, document type and
multi-label tags. It doesn't generate text or answer questions. It is
English-focused; `fastino/GLiNER2.5-multi-Decide` is the multilingual sibling
(`CLASSIFY_MODEL=… ./classify-server.sh install`).

- **Install:** `gliner2[local]` (the bare package has no torch or transformers).
- **Runs on:** Apple's GPU (MPS). ~1.2 GB RAM, loads in ~4.5 s at startup.
- **Speed:** ~50 ms per warm call; a batch of 64 texts takes ~0.6 s.
- **Concurrency:** requests run one at a time.

```bash
curl -s http://__SERVER_NAME__.__TAILNET__:8892/v1/classify -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{
  "text": "Can I get a refund for the double charge?",
  "tasks": {"intent": ["refund_request", "bug_report", "other"],
            "route": {"labels": ["billing", "support", "engineering"], "multi_label": true, "cls_threshold": 0.4}},
  "include_confidence": true}'
```

- **Request:** send `text` (→ `result`) or `texts` (up to 256, → `results`).
  Several tasks in one call share one forward pass.
- **Options:** `threshold` (0.5), `include_confidence`, `long` (chunks text past
  the encoder window), `batch_size` (8).
- **Responses:** without confidence a single-label task returns a string;
  with confidence it returns `{label, confidence}`.

### ocr-server

Surya 2 OCR (`datalab-to/surya-ocr-2`, 650M params, Q4 GGUF) as a tailnet API, for agents and any client.
Reads Sinhala and Tamil where PDF text layers and general LLMs fail. The service supervises llama.cpp
`llama-server` (Homebrew) on `127.0.0.1:8124` with `--alias datalab-to/surya-ocr-2` (surya's client sends that
model name) and runs surya's full-page recognition (`RecognitionPredictor(full_page=True)`) one page at a time.

- **Request:** `POST /v1/ocr?pages=1-3&format=text|html&dpi=192`, body = raw file bytes; or JSON
  `{"file": "<base64 or data: URL>", "pages", "format", "dpi", "filename"}`. PDF vs image comes from the
  file header (`%PDF-`), never the name.
  Returns `pages: [{page, text}]`, `seconds`, `startup_seconds`. Max 100 pages and 100 MB per call;
  one job at a time, 4 queued (429 beyond).
- **Model files:** `ocr-server/models/` holds hard links to `~/repos/sinhala-ocr/.models/` (no extra disk);
  `install` downloads them when that copy is missing.
- **Measured 2026-10-18:** model start ~1 s; a Sinhala letter page 11 s, an English form page 16.6 s.
- **Logs:** service `~/Library/Logs/ocr-server.log`, llama-server `~/Library/Logs/ocr-backend.log`.
- **dsh:** tool `ocr_document` (`clients/dsh/ocr-document.mjs`), available to every model incl. Ornith.
  Results over 40,000 characters are saved to `ocr/<name>.txt` in the workspace.
- A surya inference failure (it logs a warning and returns an empty page) becomes HTTP 502, not empty text.

### Surya 2 OCR (sinhala-ocr)

`datalab-to/surya-ocr-2`, a 650M-parameter OCR vision-language model, as a Q4 GGUF
(`.models/surya-2.gguf` 1.27 GB + `surya-2-mmproj.gguf` 205 MB) served by llama.cpp `llama-server` on
Metal. It lives in `~/repos/sinhala-ocr` on the Studio, a pipeline that OCRs Sinhala government letters
(SLMC appointment, grade and reporting letters) and extracts their dates. A date is only reported if it
appears in the OCR text, with the surrounding text stored as evidence.

- **Server:** binds `127.0.0.1:8123`, one slot (`--parallel 1`), 64k context. Not published to the tailnet.
  A `convert` job starts it; a detached watchdog kills it after 900 s with no request, so the model is not
  resident between jobs. `--keep-server` on `convert` reuses a warm server.
- **Studio:** `bin/sinhala-ocr convert in --out out` (resumable: skips docs with a `.done` marker),
  `bin/sinhala-ocr dates --out out` (rebuild `out/dates.json`, no model), `bin/sinhala-ocr doctor`.
- **MacBook:** `bin/macos/run.sh start|tail|status|fetch` rsyncs `~/Downloads/PDF` up, runs the batch and
  fetches results to `~/Downloads/PDF-ocr`.
- **Output** per document `out/<PDF stem>/`: `text.txt` (`===== PAGE n =====` markers), `blocks.json`
  (raw Surya page dumps), `.done` (sha256, pages, seconds).
- **Settings:** `SINHALA_OCR_PORT` (8123), `SINHALA_OCR_IDLE` (900), `SINHALA_OCR_CTX` (65536),
  `SINHALA_OCR_STARTUP` (300), `LLAMA_CPP_BINARY`, `--dpi` (192).
- **Corpus:** 2,489 PDFs in `in/`, all with results in `out/` (as of 2026-10-18).
- **Never kill its `llama-server`** to free memory; it is the user's job.

### Malformed tool calls (dsh)

Splash's model often fuses argument names with their values or with each
other in dsh (`formatmp3`, `bpm85`, `instrumentaltrue`,
`"filename audio/x.mp3": 20`), sometimes on the first call of a session.
The media tools share `~/.dsh/plugins/tool-args.mjs`, which splits and
validates those keys. The first malformed call in a session is rejected with
the corrected JSON to resend; later ones go ahead with the repaired arguments
and a `Note:` in the result. If an agent loops anyway, start a fresh session:
broken calls left in the history get copied. More memory won't help.

### studio-guard (dsh)

`~/.dsh/plugins/studio-guard.mjs` denies any dsh tool call that reaches this
server (`ssh`, `scp` or `tailscale ssh` to its IP or name, or `studio.sh run`)
and contains a stop, kill or restart verb: `kill`/`pkill`/`killall`, `launchctl`
bootout/stop, `shutdown`/`reboot`, `colima stop`, `docker … down/stop/kill/rm`,
`image-server.sh stop/restart/unload`, `searxng.sh down`, `tailscale down`,
`rm -r`, or `/v1/unload`. Read-only checks pass. It was added after a dsh agent
ran `pkill -f splash` to free memory for an image job. It covers dsh agents
only: in Claude Code, the 'ask first' rules below still apply.

## Common mistakes

- **Freeing memory by stopping something.** An image-server 503 means: report the numbers to the user and stop. Never kill Splash or the user's own jobs (e.g. an OCR `llama-server`) to make room.
- **Deleting or overwriting files, or stopping services, without asking.** The Studio is shared infrastructure, so confirm these first. Read-only checks such as `health`, `status` and `logs` are fine without asking.
- **Trusting `brew services list`.** It can show Colima as `stopped` while the VM is running. Check with `colima status`.
- **Downloading from Hugging Face.** The xet backend stalls at 0 bytes on the Studio: use
  `HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET=1 hf download …` (~5–7 MB/s). The HF token is in `~/.cache/huggingface/token`.
  Run long downloads with nohup: `studio.sh` gives up after 300 s.
- **`studio.sh copy` (scp) can hang** with no prompt. Push a small file instead with
  `studio.sh run "echo '<base64>' | base64 -d > FILE"` and check `shasum` on both ends.
- **Installing packages.** Use `HOMEBREW_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1 NONINTERACTIVE=1 brew install …`. A `brew install` that waits for input hangs the SSH session.
- **Opening SearXNG to the internet.** Its bot limiter is off because it's reached only from the tailnet. Never bind it to `0.0.0.0` or enable `tailscale funnel`. The same goes for OpenViking.
- **Blaming the MacBook's DNS when a `…ts.net` URL fails.** Check the Studio's current name first: `tailscale status --json` → the peer's `DNSName`. See Machine.
- **Editing this skill, `studio.sh`, `splash-settings.json` or `claude-splash.zsh` in place only.** `~/searxng-stack/install.sh` renders them from templates in that repo. Update the matching `.tmpl` too, and check that `./install.sh --dry-run` reports them as unchanged.
